## Intuition
A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has exactly two divisors: 1 and the number itself.
To check whether a number is prime, we can find all the divisors of the number and analyze their sum. If the only divisors are 1 and n, then the sum of divisors will be equal to n + 1. Using this observation, we iterate through all numbers from 1 to n and add the divisors of n to a sum variable, then compare the sum with n + 1 to determine if the number is prime.

## Approach
We iterate from 1 to n and check whether each number divides n exactly using the modulus operator (n % i == 0).
If a number divides n, it is a divisor and is added to the sum. After the loop, we compare the sum of all divisors with n + 1.
For a prime number, the only divisors are 1 and n, so the sum will be 1 + n. If the sum equals n + 1, the number is prime; otherwise, it is not prime.
